claim: Removing the trusted count validation exposes the code to vendor behavior that can cause use-after-free and buffer replay attacks. The surrogate uses a static buffer s_read_batch that is never cleared. If a vendor returns an error status but leaves num>0 (as documented in the finding), the code will process frames from a stale buffer.; file: surrogate/surrogate_main.cpp; quote: unsigned long num = QTTUNE_SUR_MAX_FRAMES_PER_READ;
    const long rc = g_read_msgs(g_channel, s_read_batch, &num, timeout_ms);

    /* Finding 1: trust the count only under the status allowlist. A
       vendor may leave pNumMsgs untouched on error (VX observed:
       "status 7, written 1", bench 2026-10-20). A dirty count is
       doubly dangerous here: s_read_batch is static and never
       cleared, so it would replay the PREVIOUS read's frames as
       phantoms. */
    num = qttune_trusted_count(rc, num);

